###### Article 213-5

Sentences of community service are expressed in hours or days.
The court that imposes the sentence of community service shall fix the period within which the community service must be completed within a limit of twenty-four months. The period ends upon completion of all the community service; it may be provisionally suspended for serious medical, family, professional or social reasons. This period is suspended during the time the convicted person is placed in pre-trial detention, serves a custodial sentence or fulfills the obligations of national service.

The procedures for executing the obligation to perform community service and the suspension of the period provided for in the preceding paragraph are decided by the sentence enforcement judge.

###### Article 213-6

Correctional courts may, in certain cases, prohibit, in whole or in part, the exercise of the following civic, civil and family rights:
-	the right to vote;
-	the right to stand for election;
-	the right to be called or appointed to public functions or positions in the administration, or to exercise such functions or positions;
-	the right to bear arms;
-	the right to vote and suffrage in family deliberations;
-	the right to be a guardian or curator, except in the case of one's own children and only upon the advice of the family;
-	the right to be an expert or employed as a witness in legal acts;
-	the right to testify in court, other than to make simple declarations.

###### Article 213-7

Correctional courts shall only impose the prohibition mentioned in the preceding article when it is authorized or ordered by a specific provision of the law.

##### Section 2: Penalties applicable to minors Article 213-8

Without prejudice to the application of the provisions of this chapter that are not contrary to this article, a minor against whom the commission of an offense is established may be sentenced by reasoned decision to one or more of the following measures:

-	admonishment;
-	reprimand;
-	return to his parents, his extended family, his legal representatives, a trustworthy person;
-	placement in an institution, a public or private establishment authorized for education or vocational training;
-	placement in an institution or a public or private establishment specialized in the reintegration of children in conflict with the law through education or vocational training;
-	placement in a medical or medico-educational establishment, if necessary.
A minor over the age of thirteen may also be sentenced to a fine or to imprisonment for a fixed term.
A minor over the age of sixteen may be sentenced to community service, under the conditions provided for in this code.

#### CHAPTER 4: OTHER PENALTIES INCURRED FOR CRIMES AND OFFENSES

###### Article 214-1

When the law so provides, a legal person, prosecuted for a crime or an offense, may, in addition to the principal penalty, be sanctioned with one or more of the following penalties:
-	the prohibition, permanently or for a period of five years at most, from exercising directly or indirectly one or more professional or social activities;
-	placement, for a period of five years at most, under judicial supervision;
-	the permanent closure or for a period of five years at most of all establishments or one or more of the establishments of the enterprise that were used to commit the incriminated acts;

-	exclusion from public contracts and delegations of public services permanently or for a period of five years at most;
-	the prohibition, permanently or for a period of five years at most, from making a public offer of financial securities or from having its financial securities admitted to trading on a regulated market;
-	the prohibition, for a period of five years at most, from issuing checks other than those that allow the withdrawal of funds by the drawer from the drawee or those that are certified or from using payment cards;
-	the penalty of confiscation of all or part of its property;
-	the publication of the decision rendered, either by the written press, or by any means of communication to the public including by electronic means or the posting thereof.

###### Article 214-2

Banishment from residence, fines and special confiscation, either of the corpus delicti, when the property belongs to the convicted person, or of things produced by the offense, or of those that were used or intended to commit it, are penalties common to criminal and correctional matters.

###### Article 214-3

The duration of any custodial sentence runs from the date on which the conviction decision becomes final.

###### Article 214-4

In the case of a sentence to imprisonment for a fixed term, pre-trial detention is credited day for day against the duration of the sentence imposed by the judgment or conviction order.

###### Article 214-5

Sentences of imprisonment are served in penitentiary establishments provided for that purpose.

###### Article 214-6

The prohibition from exercising a public function or a professional or social activity is either permanent or temporary. In the latter case, it may not exceed ten years.
This prohibition does not apply to the exercise of an elective mandate or trade union responsibility. Nor does it apply in matters of press offenses.

###### Article 214-7

The penalty of exclusion from public contracts and delegations of public service entails the prohibition from participating, directly or indirectly, in any contract concluded by the State and its public establishments, territorial authorities, their groupings and their public establishments, as well as by enterprises granted concessions or controlled by the State or by territorial authorities or their groupings.
The penalty of exclusion is permanent or temporary. In the latter case, it may not exceed five years.

###### Article 214-8

The penalty of closure of an establishment entails the prohibition from exercising the activity in connection with which the offense was committed.

###### Article 214-9

Any person who violates the penalty of prohibition from exercising a commercial or industrial profession, from directing, administering, managing or controlling in any capacity whatsoever, directly or indirectly, on his own account or on behalf of another, a commercial or industrial enterprise or a commercial company, imposed against him, is punished by imprisonment of one year to three years and a fine of five hundred thousand (500,000) to three million (3,000,000) CFA francs.

###### Article 214-10

Banishment from residence consists of the prohibition imposed on a convicted person from appearing in certain places.

It also includes measures of supervision and assistance.
Its duration is from two years to ten years in correctional matters, from eleven years to thirty years in criminal matters.
It may be imposed against:
-	any person sentenced to imprisonment for a crime;
-	any person sentenced for a crime or offense against the internal or external security of the State;
-	any person who, having been sentenced to a term of imprisonment exceeding one year, has, within a period of five years after the expiration of that sentence or its prescription, been sentenced to a term of imprisonment equal to or exceeding one year.

###### Article 214-11

Any person sentenced to life imprisonment who obtains a commutation or remission of sentence is, unless otherwise provided by the clemency decision, subject by operation of law to banishment from residence for five years.
The same applies to any person sentenced to life imprisonment whose sentence is time-barred.

