###### Article 331-1

For the purposes of this title, the following terms mean:
- Public official:
• any civilian or military person who holds a legislative, executive, administrative or judicial mandate, whether appointed or elected, on a permanent or temporary basis, whether remunerated or not and regardless of their hierarchical level or seniority;
• any other civilian or military person vested with a function or mandate, even temporary, remunerated or not, who contributes, in that capacity, to the service of a public body or public enterprise, or any other enterprise in which the State holds all or part of its capital, or any other enterprise that provides a public service;
• the agent of any legal person under private law responsible for the execution of a public service or public procurement regardless of the terms of the mission entrusted to them;
• any other civilian or military person defined as a public official or assimilated thereto in accordance with the legislation and regulations in force;

- foreign public official: any civilian or military person who holds a legislative, executive, administrative or judicial mandate with a foreign country, whether appointed or elected; and any civilian or military person who performs a public function for a foreign country, including for a public body or public enterprise;
- enrichment balance: the statement of movable and immovable acquisitions during a given period balanced against the legal income obtained or legal means mobilized that enabled these acquisitions;
- property: all types of assets, corporeal or incorporeal, movable or immovable, tangible or intangible, as well as legal instruments or documents attesting to the ownership of these assets or the rights relating thereto;
- gift: a present, an object given to a person with the intention of pleasing them;
- confiscation: the permanent deprivation of property by decision of a judicial body or any other competent authority;
- conflict of interest: any situation of interference between a public interest and public or private interests that is likely to influence or appear to influence the independent, impartial and objective exercise of a function;
- Convention: the United Nations Convention against Corruption;
- donation: the act of voluntarily transferring something to a person without requesting anything in exchange;
- entity: an organized set of corporeal or incorporeal elements or natural or legal persons, pursuing its own objective;
- official of a public international organization: any international official or any person authorized by such an organization to act on its behalf;
- freezing or seizure: the temporary prohibition of the transfer, conversion, disposition or movement of property, or the temporary assumption of custody or control of property by decision of a court or other competent authority;

- conventional hospitality: any disinterested act generally accepted in social relations and falling within the customs of society;
- predicate offence: any offence as a result of which proceeds are generated that may become the subject of money laundering in accordance with the applicable legislation relating thereto;
- controlled delivery: the method of allowing the exit from national territory, passage through the territory or entry into the territory of illicit or suspected illicit shipments, with the knowledge and under the control of the competent authorities, in order to investigate an offence and identify the persons involved in its commission;
- assets: all movable and immovable property, rights and claims belonging to the declarant and related persons but also the debts and financial commitments contracted by the latter;
- related person: any person related to the declarant by the bonds of marriage, de facto union, filiation or adoption, with the exception of adult children.
- proceeds of crime: any property derived, directly or indirectly, from the commission of an offence or obtained, directly or indirectly, by committing it.
- misuse of the single-source or direct agreement procedure: exceeding the quota set by WAEMU regulations and awarding more than one contract by the single-source or direct agreement procedure either to a natural or legal person, or to close relatives up to the 4th degree in the direct line and the 5th in the collateral line in the same year.

###### Article 331-2

Is punishable by a term of imprisonment of two years to five years and a fine equal to twice the value of the promises accepted, undue advantages or things received or requested, provided that said fine is not less than two million (2,000,000) CFA francs:
- anyone who promises, offers or grants to a public official, directly or indirectly, an undue advantage, either for himself or for another

person or entity, so that he performs or refrains from performing an act in the exercise of his functions;
- any public official who solicits or accepts, directly or indirectly, an undue advantage, for himself or for another person or entity, in order to perform or refrain from performing an act within his functions.

#### CHAPTER 2: RELATED OFFENCES

##### Section 1: Unjustified advantages and corruption in public procurement

###### Article 332-1

Is punishable by a term of imprisonment of five years to ten years and a fine of two million (2,000,000) to ten million (10,000,000) CFA francs:
- any public official who enters into, endorses or revises a contract, agreement, public procurement or amendment in violation of the legislative and regulatory provisions in force in order to procure an unjustified advantage for another;
- any merchant, industrialist, artist or artisan, private sector entrepreneur or in general, any natural or legal person who enters into, even occasionally, a contract or public procurement with the State, local authorities, public law establishments or bodies and State companies by taking advantage of the authority or influence of the agents of the aforementioned bodies to increase the prices they normally and usually charge or to modify, to their advantage, the quality of goods or services or delivery or supply deadlines.

###### Article 332-2

Is punishable by the penalties provided for in Article 332-1 above, anyone who compels or attempts to compel by force or threats, corrupts or attempts to corrupt by promise, offer, donation or present, a public official, whether or not the attempt was followed by effect.

###### Article 332-3

Is punishable by a term of imprisonment of two years to five years and a fine equivalent to three times the amount or value of the advantage received or to be received, any public official who, on the occasion of the preparation, negotiation, conclusion or execution of a public procurement, contract or amendment concluded in the name of the State or local authorities, public establishments of the State or State companies, receives or attempts to receive, directly or indirectly, for his benefit or for the benefit of a third party, remuneration or an advantage of any nature whatsoever from a private contractor.

